WEBVTT

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:11.190
A horse is a horse, of course, of course.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.310
And no one can talk to a horse, of course.

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:17.070
That is, of course, unless the horse is the famous Mr. Ed.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:19.710
Write to the source and ask the horse.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.750
He'll give you the answer that you endorse.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:23.970
He's always on a steady course.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.710
Talk to Mr. Ed.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.730
It said flour, baking powder, and salt together.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.260
Carol, Carol, would you like to hear the speech I wrote?

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.060
What speech?

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:44.250
Well, you know, the one I'm giving to the canvassers at the Treadway Iron and Steel Company.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:44.710
Hmm?

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.500
Mr. Treadway, he wants me to show them how to sell a payroll savings plan to their fellow employees.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:54.090
I'm supposed to talk on the subject of designing your future on account of my being an architect.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:55.500
You get it, designing?

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:56.880
I get it.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.960
It's pretty clever, too, but you're a little out of practice about...

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:03.450
Payroll savings? We haven't bought a savings bond in a long time.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:08.290
Ever since I sold the board of directors on Mr. Treadway's pet plan for the new administration building,

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:09.630
he thinks I'm a great orator.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:12.890
You must be. You sold me on marrying you.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:14.810
Go ahead, honey, read me your speech.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:25.950
Fellow America, the job you are about to undertake is one which will benefit both your co-workers and your country.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.930
It is a public service of the highest order.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:30.800
You're not listening, Carol.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:31.900
Yes, I am. Let me go on.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:32.180
Oh.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:38.180
It is a privilege to address you on the importance of the payroll savings plan for the purchase of savings bonds.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:42.090
This plan is a boon to all Americans because, let us be honest,

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.760
when a man brings home his hard-earned money at the end of the week, what does he do with it?

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:48.890
Pour into an ungreased pan.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:50.860
He pours it into an ungreased pan.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:58.850
I say this in all sincerity.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:04.490
If the worker will take time to beat his yolks well and add the necessary hot milk

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.940
and bake in a moderate oven at 330 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.640
then he will realize the goal of every loyal citizen

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.050
the day when he can retire to his own little sponge cake

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.370
and live happily between two layers.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:19.640
I thank you. What do you think?

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.200
Sounds wonderful, honey, but don't you think you could cut it down a little?

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:24.940
You mean make it one layer?

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:25.960
Hmm?

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.970
Well, I bet Lincoln's wife wasn't baking a sponge cake when he was trying out...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.760
his gettysburg address on her and if mrs washington didn't help george out with his speech

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:41.060
that farewell address might never have gone beyond the two words goodbye fellas well what on earth

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:48.020
are you talking about oh never mind i'll just go back to the barn and try my speech out on my horse

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:53.700
oh wilbur have you called the electrician to have him come fix a short now bridge lamp yeah he'll be

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:59.450
here right after lunch thanks carol you

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.730
Would you hear my speech after lunch, Carol?

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:05.550
I heard you, darling.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.190
The electrician will be here after lunch.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.400
Yeah, I hope your sponge cake drops, honey.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:12.140
Thank you, darling.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:12.700
You're welcome.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:23.020
The payroll savings plan offers the worker an opportunity

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.220
to accumulate savings bonds on a regular basis.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:29.680
Savings are done for him by his company each payday.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:31.920
How's it sound so far, Ed?

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:33.580
Pretty bad.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:35.890
You could do any better?

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.280
If I couldn't, I'd give up talking.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:41.620
What don't you like about this, Pete?

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:43.900
Don't sound sincere.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:45.050
Don't?

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.460
How many bonds do you own, Wilbur?

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.300
You mean savings bonds?

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:53.900
We're not talking about watermelons.

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:57.010
Well, I don't own any at the present time, Ed.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:59.470
You see, when I went in business for myself...

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.580
Well, I went off my company's payroll savings plan.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.480
And you cashed the bond you had, huh?

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:05.900
Yeah.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.160
Well, if Carol and I needed the cash for a down payment on this house,

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:10.730
you would have done the same thing.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.140
I doubt it.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.120
I'm eccentric.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.920
I prefer living in a stable.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.900
And I don't have to own savings bonds to be a bond salesman.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:20.490
Besides, I told you,

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:24.220
I used to have a lot of savings bonds before I started my own business.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:27.740
And I used to have a lot of hay before I started lunch.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.930
Ah, what does a horse know about savings?

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:38.610
bonds i just know your speech doesn't sound sincere what can i do about it buy bonds ed

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:46.470
will you stop nagging right now i just don't have any real need for savings bonds okay but how about

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:53.620
the future you know someday we'll both want to retire so what so savings bonds are one of the

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:59.720
surest safest ways of saving you know that i know they're backed by the government you

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:10.190
always get what you put in plus interest you can't lose right but no buts about it and consider these

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:17.500
points savings bonds are just like cash meaning they can be redeemed quickly and easily at your

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:26.420
bank are you finished not yet savings bonds are also loss proof meaning if stolen lost or destroyed

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:29.640
your bonds are replaced by the government

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:30.760
Are you finished?

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:36.220
Finally, the payroll savings plan is the most painless way of saving ever invented.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.440
You never miss those few dollars out of each paycheck

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:44.180
because the saving is done for you before you ever see it.

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:46.010
Now I'm finished.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:49.670
Nice speech, Ed. Nice speech.

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.090
Well, anyway, it's sincere.

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:55.130
How could it be when you don't have any bonds, wise guy?

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.520
You mean I don't sound sincere?

00:05:58.780 --> 00:05:59.780
Of course not.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:01.650
You made that point to me, remember?

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.540
And what's good for the boss is good for the horse.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:13.640
Hey, wonder if they sell savings bonds to horses.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:39.660
Operator, get me Washington, D.C., Treasury Department.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:43.310
Yeah, I'll pay.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.000
Carol, can you spare a few minutes to hear my speech?

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:54.450
Soon as I put the cake in the oven, honey.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:55.200
Okay.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:10.810
Thank you. Thank you.

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:13.050
Oh, please, not too much.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:13.990
You're spoiling me.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:16.260
That is too much.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:17.570
Thank you.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:19.020
Thank you.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:19.540
Thank you.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:22.940
Chairman, fellow citizens,

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:25.670
it is a privilege to address you on the subject

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:27.550
of designing your future.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:29.960
Believe me in building...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.180
a family's financial plan, just as in building a skyscraper, the foundation is all important.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:42.270
So, when you advise your fellow workers to build that plan around United States savings

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:46.080
bonds, you are giving them the best possible advice.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:54.900
Savings bonds are a solid rock of security, a launching pad for those dreams of the future.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.560
Hey, that's pretty cute, Mr. Post.

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.200
I like that launching pad angle.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:04.400
I wish I'd said it.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:05.670
Oh, hi, Mr. Davis.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:06.550
Did you fix the lamp?

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:07.820
Oh, don't worry about that.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:08.280
It'll live.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:09.500
Hey, what's with the speech?

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:10.260
You going on TV?

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:13.600
Well, I'm giving a talk to the payroll savings canvassers

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:14.860
at Treadway Iron and Steel.

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:18.140
You know, pep them up, inspire them to be good bond salesmen.

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:19.880
Ah, I got news for you, Mr. Post.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.340
Making a speech won't do the job.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:21.860
I know.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.700
I was a canvasser myself in the last drive at our shop.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:25.800
You know what you got to do?

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:26.760
You got to give them the answers.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:28.280
You got to tell them how to meet objections.

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:29.580
Objections?

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:29.940
Sure.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.980
Oh, saving don't come easy to most people.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:34.840
They'll give you 87 excuses for not signing up.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:37.360
So, the canvasser's got to have 88 answers.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:38.870
I'll show you what.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:43.150
Now look, you be the canvasser, and I'll be the guy in the job, and you try and sign me up.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:45.790
Mr. Davis, I'm paying you by the hour.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:46.970
I can't afford the lecture.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:48.730
All right, forget it.

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.870
But when you fall flat on your face, remember you heard it here first.

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:55.850
Well, maybe you could give me a short course.

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:57.090
You know, just tell me the question.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:59.960
Al, like a...

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.790
guy I'll say I can't afford to save very good point what do I tell him tell him look Charlie

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:12.100
you can't afford to be sick or off the job either but it happens and you better be ready for that kind

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:16.570
of an emergency when it comes up with saving bonds in the sock and then maybe he'll tell you

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:23.050
they already got too many deducts off their check that's a tough one to answer for you but not for

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:29.970
me I say this is a deduct you take right home with you only it's in the shape of a bond this is

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:34.840
take home pay too and the only kind that grows that's what i tell them when you're canvassing

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:43.980
you got to be a real live wire well that's easy for you you're an electrician yeah then they'll

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:49.810
tell you i can save better some other ways and i say sure sure you can save any way you want but

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:54.260
you'll never find an easier way than this when the saving is done for you before you even get your

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:59.320
hands on the money bonds are just like cash when you need it but if you let them lay they'll keep

00:09:59.320 --> 00:09:59.760
growing.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.540
the important things you'll need someday and not only that but you can save on your taxes because

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:10.770
you don't have to pay income tax on the interest until you cash the bonds you might even be retired

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:15.790
by then and you won't have much to pay you know that's something i didn't know you mind if i take

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:24.540
a few notes i don't mind if you make a movie out of it mr post i got a hunch that you don't know

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:29.640
too much about payroll savings i mean being a big shot architect and all you know i

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.740
I want you to know, Mr. Davis, that I was in the payroll savings plan myself for many years

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:36.380
before I went into business on my own.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:38.020
I know all about it.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:39.620
That's why they picked me to make the speech.

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:40.240
Yeah.

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:45.330
Well, no offense, Mr. Post, but you sound like a guy just making a speech, you know?

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:46.520
No oomph.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:47.580
Oomph?

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:47.940
Right.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:48.720
No oomph.

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:51.970
Like a guy that maybe isn't using the product himself.

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:53.520
You got any bonds?

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:57.130
Well, now, that's rather a personal question, Mr. Davis.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:10:59.620
When I discuss the matter with my horse...

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:02.800
Any horse will tell you to say horse.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:06.960
Yeah, well, I was rehearsing my speech in front of him,

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:08.000
the way I was with you,

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:11.300
and he doesn't know half as much as you do about payroll savings.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:13.760
Oh, well, he hasn't had a chance to study, he's...

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.160
Oh, come on, you got me talking like you.

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:18.670
Tell me some more of the questions you get, Mr. Davis.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:21.220
Well, there's the one where they say,

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:24.910
I'd like to sign up, but I got a lot of bills to pay.

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:28.100
I should think you'd tell him, sign up now,

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:29.980
and we'll date the card ahead to whenever you say...

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:30.460
Hey, how's that?

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:33.310
As an electrician, I hate to say this, Mr. Post.

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:35.430
But you are now cooking with gas.

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:36.550
Thank you.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:39.580
I bet between us, we'd have them eating out of our hands.

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:43.240
I'd do okay, but I hate to say this, Mr. Post.

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:44.930
You don't have it here.

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:46.980
On account of you ain't a payroll saver.

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:48.540
You know, folks can always tell.

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:50.810
But I used to be on payroll savings.

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:53.370
We bought a lot of bonds while we bought this house with them.

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:55.680
Yeah, but what have you done about it lately?

00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:57.670
It's not my fault.

00:11:57.770 --> 00:11:58.850
I'm my own boss now.

00:11:59.400 --> 00:11:59.980
I can't...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.260
I can't get on payroll savings.

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.680
Oh, I'm bleeding for you, Mr. Post.

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:04.920
I'm bleeding for you.

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:10.340
Tell you what, Mr. Post, you give me your house and your business,

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:11.660
and I'll give you my job.

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:12.920
Very funny.

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:17.190
To hear you talk, you'd think that payroll savings was the only way you could buy bonds.

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:18.790
Don't you ever go to the bank?

00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:19.790
Certainly.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:21.470
To pick up a free calendar, maybe?

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:25.640
Don't you know your bank's got savings bonds on sale at all times?

00:12:26.090 --> 00:12:29.130
Why, they'd even let you sign up to buy a bond a month automatically.

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:30.890
out of your checking account.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:41.260
Well, excuse me, Mr. Post, let that be light.

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:51.810
I don't feel too bad.

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:53.910
Edison didn't hit it the first time either.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:57.480
Well, like the guys at the office always say,

00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:59.880
I'm a great barn salesman, but the world's worst.

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:29.910
electrician. I think I'm going to ask Mr. Treadway to get.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.940
somebody else to make that bond speech why well i thought i knew all about savings bonds but

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:42.260
seems everybody else knows more about it than i do don't say that honey would you like to try

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:48.090
your speech out on me again what do you mean 20 minutes ago i lost a close decision to your

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:56.920
sponge cake darling i'm sorry i should have paid more attention ah you didn't miss a thing i'm

00:13:56.920 --> 00:13:59.670
a pretty terrible salesman now wilbur don't say that

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:02.480
I couldn't sell a rowboat to a drowning man.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:03.420
Now stop that.

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:05.420
I will not have you knocking my husband.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:06.580
You know what I think?

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:07.160
What?

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:08.770
You have a guilty conscience.

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:12.580
Ever since we cashed our savings bonds to buy this house,

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:16.340
we've been spending every last cent and never saving anything.

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:18.830
That's what you get from marrying a sport.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:20.380
I wanted you to have things.

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:21.900
Silly.

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:24.760
You can have things and still save regularly.

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:27.820
All a woman really wants is security.

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:29.710
Plus a loving husband, of course.

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:31.710
Even if he can't make a decent speech?

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:33.700
You can make a fine speech.

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:36.070
All you need is to be sincere.

00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:37.030
Oh.

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:40.160
You sound just like that electrician.

00:14:42.890 --> 00:14:45.050
He says if you're not buying bonds yourself,

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:47.050
you can't convince other people they should buy them.

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:47.810
He's right.

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:51.380
I have to confess something to you, honey.

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:55.620
I heard you and Mr. Davis talking about the payroll savings plan in there.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:59.050
You know, all the arguments people give for not being able to save,

00:14:59.150 --> 00:14:59.980
and all the...

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.780
answers he had. Why, it's the first time I've ever been sold anything by remote

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:10.690
control. You know, I think we should sign up for a regular bond every month. Well,

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:14.840
there's one thing about my little wife. When she's right, she's right. And when

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:20.830
she's wrong, she's right. And this time, I'm wrong and you're right. We'll start

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:24.990
buying bonds again. Oh, honey, and then you can do your canvassing with sincerity.

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:28.890
You know, give it a little oomph. Wait till I tell that electrician.

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:37.920
Well, fix the light, Mr. Post.

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:40.920
Well, now fix my nose.

00:15:42.250 --> 00:15:46.460
Oh, cake.

00:16:00.670 --> 00:16:03.550
Oh, it's sponge cake. Look at it. It's flat.

00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:08.490
Well, honey, as long as we have savings bonds, we'll never be flat.

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:15.100
What do you mean horses can't own savings bonds?

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:17.450
What are they? Animals?

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:19.790
You listen to me, fella.

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:22.750
Take it easy, Ed.

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:24.850
The nerve of that guy.

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:29.210
Suppose we horses passed a law that people couldn't ride horses.

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:29.930
Ed, listen.

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:32.700
Horses can't own savings bonds.

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:34.340
A cat I could understand.

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:35.760
Relax, Ed.

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:39.340
Carol and I are going to be buying savings bonds regularly through our bank.

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:42.840
And as a member of the family, you will be sharing in the pride of ownership.

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:45.520
You feel better now?

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:46.820
Yes, sir.

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:49.640
But you can't blame me for feeling hurt, Wilbur.

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:52.140
After all, horses are human, too.

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:59.970
Friends, I hope you enjoyed our special...

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.440
Mr. Ed's story for United States Savings Bonds

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:04.920
half as much as we enjoyed doing it for you.

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:08.880
But now for a minute, I'd like to be myself, Alan Young,

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:11.820
to thank you on behalf of the Treasury Department

00:17:11.820 --> 00:17:13.500
for the important work you are doing

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:16.250
as canvassers for the payroll savings plan.

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:19.740
And let me remind you that the service you are performing

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:22.240
for your co-workers is one they will never forget.

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:26.080
We all have a way of remembering the nice things in our lives

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:27.840
and the people who are associated with them,

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:29.960
maybe signing somebody up for payroll savings.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:35.200
year old savings is not quite the same as forming a marriage ceremony or delivering a young couple's

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:41.360
first baby but just the same learning to save is an important milestone in any family's life

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:47.550
and every payday as their bonds keep piling up the people you signed up will feel grateful

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:53.860
all over again your country is grateful too because savings bonds are good for america

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:59.880
an investment in our free way of life so for everyone in our

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.920
cast, Connie Hines, who plays the part of Carol, George O'Hanlon, who as Mr. Davis

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:11.320
was the electrician and super bond salesman, and of course, Mr. Ed, who has authorized

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:12.300
me to speak for him.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:13.520
Thanks again.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:14.610
Good luck.
